why: the two boxes still open under BACKLOG's `gate-honesty`. They are
  the tax every lane pays: the gate cried wolf four times in one day,
  and the real cost ran the other way — that session nearly dismissed
  a GENUINE defect (actor-stop-drain, 219 of 300 messages lost) as
  load, by analogy with these.

    suite                          in the gate   alone
    okay.kafka.TestKafkaInterop        237 s     0.084 s
    okay.intent.TestCutStops            39 s     0.059 s
    okay.ui TestSwing (the law)      timeout     passes
    okay.persist.TestStable      BindException   0.007 s

WHAT I HAVE READ ALREADY, before claiming, because the entry insists
each needs its OWN reading of whether its assertion depends on timing:

  - TestCutStops asserts TOKEN COUNTS and emission counts. No clock,
    no deadline, nothing timing-dependent.
  - TestKafkaInterop is mocks (`MockConsumer`), asserting values and
    their order. No clock either.
  - TestSwing's law renders twelve frames and compares each against a
    freshly built one. No clock.
  - TestStable's second test asserts `currentTerm == 5` and
    `votedFor == Some("1")`. It is not about networking at all; the
    port is incidental machinery, and it is bound the textbook wrong
    way:

        val port = { val ss = ServerSocket(0); val p = ss.getLocalPort; ss.close(); p }
        val node = RaftWire.Node("0", port, ...)

    ask the OS, CLOSE it, then race a neighbour to bind it again.
    `RaftWire.Node` passes its port straight to `ServerSocket(port)`,
    which takes 0 and lets the OS assign — so the pre-bind buys
    nothing and costs a collision.

So on the face of it all four say "the assertion does not depend on
timing", and AGENTS' first option (a longer deadline) is the honest
one for the three, with the port fixed properly for the fourth.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - 237 s against 0.084 s is 2 800x. CPU starvation on a 14-core box
    carrying six sibling builds does not multiply by 2 800. If that
    suite is SPINNING rather than starved — a poll or retry loop with
    no backoff, doing orders of magnitude more work under contention
    — then a longer deadline HIDES A DEFECT, and hiding one is
    exactly what this backlog entry exists to stop. The same question
    must be asked of the 39 s.
  - a longer deadline must be justified per suite and never swept:
    the entry says so, and a blanket `munitTimeout` across the repo
    would be the sweeping it forbids.
  - "it passes alone" proves nothing about the fix. The mechanism has
    to be reproduced, and this session already learned that a load
    harness may FAIL to reproduce it (28 spinners did not reproduce
    the ui pair) — in which case isolate the variable instead.

predict: kafka is NOT plain starvation. The ratio is too large, and I
  expect a busy poll loop — `pollMillis = 10` with a mock that
  answers instantly — that makes progress only when it wins a
  timeslice. TestCutStops and TestSwing I expect to be genuinely
  starved, deadline-only. Recorded so being wrong shows.
